  <page_break/>
  <heading level="6">
    <location value="217"/>
    <location value="48"/>
    <location value="322"/>
    <location value="56"/>
    Acknowledgments
  </heading>
  <text>
    <location value="90"/>
    <location value="64"/>
    <location value="449"/>
    <location value="168"/>
    When I was a undergraduate, due to a complex set of circumstances, I once lost interests in research and no longer believed it could become part of my career. However, Prof. Hongyu Zhao truly changed everything. I am deeply grateful that he accepted me as his summer interns and further doctoral students, and has been willing to provide me with the most generous support and the best guidance. Not only did he provide me with the utmost support in my research direction and the greatest freedom to explore, but he also took a keen interest in my personal life and character development. He taught me how to become a researcher of integrity and rigor while maintaining a deep sense of humanistic care. I am deeply grateful to Prof. Zhao and delighted to have chosen him as my mentor. He is my role model, for sure.
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="90"/>
    <location value="239"/>
    <location value="449"/>
    <location value="383"/>
<![CDATA[I'm also delighted to be working alongside my labmates at Zhao Lab. Dr. Ning Sun is just like our parents in lab. She helps me a lot in developing a sound scientific research ethos. At the same time, her questions always pinpointed the pain points in my model design and spurred me to keep thinking. She has contributed so much to our lab, and I am truly grateful to her. Besides, I am thankful to my collaborators in the lab, including Dr. Yuge Wang (who also supervised me during my summer internship and worked as a perfect mentor), Dr. Jia Zhao, Dr. Gefei Wang, Dr. Lijun Wang, Dr. Yingxin Lin, Dr. Biqing Zhu, Dr. Tinyi Chu, Wangjie Zheng, Leqi Xu, Jiaqi Hu, Xiangyu Zhang, Lisa Chen, Yueqian Jing, Yiming Shi, Feiyang Wen, and Haoran Shao. I have collaborated with them on multiple projects, ranging from integrating single-cell multi-omic data to modeling electronic health records using language models. Their experience and knowledge are invaluable assets, and I am deeply grateful for their generous support in both research and life. I also extend my gratitude to the other members of our lab and wish everyone a bright future.]]>  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="90"/>
    <location value="167"/>
    <location value="449"/>
    <location value="239"/>
<![CDATA[I would also like to thank my committee members, including Prof. Rex Ying and Prof. Yuval Kluger. They also provided me with tremendous support during my doctoral studies. Prof. Ying supported me in publishing my first paper at a top-tier machine learning conference (NeurIPS) and provided me with the most generous support during my job search stage. Prof. Kluger's insightful questions always prompt me to think more deeply, and his humor is an indispensable part of our meetings.]]>  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="90"/>
    <location value="383"/>
    <location value="449"/>
    <location value="455"/>
    My co-authors have also been an integral part of my doctoral journey, and I am deeply grateful for their support and many of them become my good friends. Without them, I would not have been able to achieve what I have today. First and foremost, I would like to thank Prof. Xiao Luo and Prof. Wengong Jin. They are not only my reliable mentors in research but also great friends in life, embodying a genuine relationship of collaborators and friends. I would also like to thank my collaborators at Yale, such as Prof. Le Zhang, Prof. Hua Xu, Prof. Qingyu Chen, Tinglin Huang
  </text>
  <page_footer>
    <layer value="furniture"/>
    <location value="265"/>
    <location value="471"/>
    <location value="274"/>
    <location value="482"/>
    iii
  </page_footer>
  <page_break/>
  <text>
    <location value="90"/>
    <location value="46"/>
    <location value="449"/>
    <location value="118"/>
    (who is also a great player in League of Legends, along with Weikang), Allen Wang, etc. as well as outside Yale, including Yijia Xiao, Haixin Wang, Fan Zhang, Dr. Artur Szalata, Tong Ding, Chuhan Li, Dr. Simeng Han, Dr. Hanchen Wang, Dr. Kexin Huang, Prof. James Zou, all teammates in SAGA and Agent4BioMed, etc. I would also like to thank my mentors from industry, including Dr. Aviv Regev and Dr. Graham Heimberg. I spent three enjoyable months at Genentech, largely thanks to their excellent guidance.
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="90"/>
    <location value="159"/>
    <location value="449"/>
    <location value="200"/>
    Finally, I would like to thank my family for supporting my decision to pursue a doctoral degree. This has been a bumpy path, but they have always been by my side, giving me their strongest support. I am fortunate to have them along with me throughout my life. I love you all!
  </text>
  <text>
    <location value="90"/>
    <location value="117"/>
    <location value="449"/>
    <location value="159"/>
    I also thank my interns and mentees, including Wenxin Long, Zhiyuan Cao, Kexing Li, Sihan Jiang, and other outstanding young researchers. I feel fortunate to have worked alongside them and witnessed their growth. Many of them are still collaborating with me, which is what touches me most deeply.
  </text>
